WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
You get one glance at a headline.

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:04.600
Who do you think did something wrong?

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.920
Hold that thought.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.040
We matched thirty-two weeks of archived
homepages.

00:00:10.152 --> 00:00:13.640
Migration appeared on fifteen BBC dates,

00:00:13.709 --> 00:00:16.920
eleven ITV dates and five Channel Four
dates.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.960
Scrutiny of Reform conduct appeared on one,
two and five.

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:24.540
Then we looked closer.

00:00:24.615 --> 00:00:28.020
Between October the sixteenth and
November the eighteenth,

00:00:28.073 --> 00:00:30.580
we matched a hundred and eleven desktop
checks.

00:00:30.729 --> 00:00:33.620
Twenty-one scheduled checks had gaps.

00:00:33.796 --> 00:00:36.340
Here’s what the first screen showed.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.720
On the BBC, applicants appeared to be
posing as experts.

00:00:40.944 --> 00:00:46.320
Four hours later, the same investigation
led with a firm offering fake art.

00:00:46.512 --> 00:00:48.200
Same story.

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.080
Who sounds responsible now?

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:55.020
The BBC had one check explicitly portraying
migrants negatively.

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.860
A broader reading gives five.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:02.060
For alleged Reform wrongdoing, the counts
were zero and one.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.760
On ITV, a Reform hopeful called for
migrants to be confined.

00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:09.560
Three checks carried that rhetoric.

00:01:09.693 --> 00:01:12.120
Whose conduct was the story questioning?

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:14.080
The speaker’s.

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:19.880
ITV had five checks scrutinising Reform and
none directly blaming migrants.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.740
Channel Four kept one undercover
investigation into foreign donations

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:26.820
on the first screen at every check.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.420
A hundred and eleven appearances of one
story.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:34.900
The headline raised suspicion without
naming a specific breach.

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.560
Next time you open the news, pick the first
headline.

00:01:38.736 --> 00:01:40.001
Find the verb.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:41.600
Who gets the blame?

00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:44.720
Would you judge another outlet by the same
rule?

00:01:44.896 --> 00:01:47.600
Play the challenge at Coding for Justice.
